27
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.720
So about this concept of distant writing,

28
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.360
I wish I could
say, let's have a show of hands.

29
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.880
How many of you know
this concept of disarray?

30
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.120
But we came across these
this idea of distant writing,

31
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.120
as articulated by Luciano Florida
in a 2025 paper

32
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.640
called Decent Writing Literary Production
in the Age of Artificial Intelligence,

33
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.600
where he kind of riffs off
of this idea of close reading, where human

34
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.680
actually reads
an interpretive, interprets a text

35
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.160
which was then taken on
by the digital humanities,

36
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.960
you know, take distant reading
as this kind of computational analysis

37
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.760
of large corpora
to detect these overarching patterns,

38
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.840
to then kind of extrapolate into the idea
of close writing or something.

39
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.800
We in one of our meetings
called legacy writing.

40
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.160
So where a human author
actually composes text directly.

41
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:30.000
So for him, then the distant writing
is this type of writing where human is

42
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.200
kind of
like the designer of narrative constraint,

43
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.520
sets requirements
through prompts and curates AI output,

44
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.240
but the LMS ultimately
are the ones who execute the text.

45
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.560
And Florida himself
has this very interesting approach

46
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.800
to using distant writing most first

47
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.160
and foremost, kind of to
as a way of dealing with abundance.

48
00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.240
Because he had this interesting idea
already a long time ago,

49
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.480
before these technologies
came to take secondary characters

50
00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.920
of known novels
and then to bring them into new narrative.

51
00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.080
But that was so much,
too much data for human cognition alone.

52
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.400
So once these tools became available, 
Florida

53
00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.400
took the secondary characters
as his data set and used

54
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.720
LMS to generate a story
that brings the secondary characters

55
00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.320
into a coherent
and meaningful interaction.

56
00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.280
In a printed book called encounters,
which is forthcoming.

57
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.800
And then we have another idea.

58
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.080
Well, the same idea.

59
00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.760
It's different words
writing at a distance, which was proposed

60
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.960
by Hans Bayern in a paper from 2024,

61
00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.160
where Bayard is basically also

62
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.840
thinking in very much along the same lines
in terms of the distance between author

63
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.840
and final text or human and final text,
let's put it that way.

64
00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.840
But he kind of takes a granular

65
00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:05.400
approach to the the degree of distance
to in order for us to be able

66
00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.440
to think about the implications of that
order of magnitude of the distance.

67
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.080
So basically, primary authorship, human
rights, the text, secondary authorship,

68
00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.800
we would have some kind of a code

69
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.440
or ruleset there
in between that determines the text.

70
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.200
Tertiary authorship
is where we would have a weighted model

71
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:32.320
and the code, but we are the ones in
control of the weighted model.

72
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.120
Quaternary authorship.

73
00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:39.920
In his Quaternary authorship in his model
is where we also have

74
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.680
like a corporate closed
proprietary interface in between.

75
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.640
So there were ceding authorship
also to the proprietary interface.

76
00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.560
The way that BioMed uses
this approach is that he trains

77
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.640
a model on four contemporary novels,
and then generates writing,

78
00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.720
which is curious, into a printed book,
which was published in 2023

79
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.800
called Berlin Miami. So

80
00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:05.720
again, distant

81
00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.880
writing with these llms is yet again
an expansion of possibilities

82
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.560
that redefines the relationship
between author, reader and text, clearly

83
00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.400
part of a long continuum of this type
of processes of redefinition.

84
00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:25.760
But what we thought
was that ultimately, this approach to

85
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.000
writing at a distance can be interpreted
and utilized in endless ways.

86
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.560
So, inspired by these ideas,
we embarked on our own experiments.

87
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.760
Where so where?

88
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:41.640
Florida and Bayard aimed for a traditional
printed book as a final product.

89
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.160
Our aim was to create a digital
reading experience.

90
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:49.920
So a text that changes where user
agency plays a role

91
00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.000
in shaping the evolution of the text
in a meaningful way.

92
00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.040
And our creative constraint
was this idea of the ripple,

93
00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:04.720
which just emerged in one of our
meetings, where we think of the ripple

94
00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:09.200
as something where the user chooses,
writes, or does something,

95
00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.680
and then that choice
somehow has to ripple through in the LM

96
00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.280
generated literary experience
in a meaningful and coherent way.

97
00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.520
And as such, our job assistant writers
is to author the system in a way

98
00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:30.480
that makes it robust for unsupervised
but meaningful interaction with the user.

99
00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.520
So onto our ripples.

100
00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.880
I'm going to show you a brief.

101
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:36.240
Yeah.

102
00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.240
So overall, the three experiments in the

103
00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:44.320
in our three distinct experience
experiments are kind of experiments

104
00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.160
in the materiality of the LM
as a writing machine,

105
00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.040
but with different takes on what
that means.

106
00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:56.880
And all of our experiments are based on

107
00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:02.480
this kind of world jockey system
prompts created by Watson at some point,

108
00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.560
which is this complex prompt that evokes
sort of this multi

109
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:13.440
plane system that allows users to steer
an interactive world much like a DJ would.

110
00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:17.280
So this is the prompt
and you can access it

111
00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.280
and play with it yourself if you like.

112
00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.880
So, you know, we define
quite a lot of things in the prompt.

113
00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:25.920
And yesterday

114
00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.160
when I put it into Gemini Canvas,
it gave me something that looks like this.

115
00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.720
So there's this idea of like a world
and some beings

116
00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.280
and then, you know,
you can click and have stuff happen.

117
00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:41.400
But what happened was that effectively,
from this shared system

118
00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.760
prompt, we kind of all rippled
