131
00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:42.520
So here we have a girl on the train
and we hear her thoughts.

132
00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.720
More pressing is sorting out
the next study group meetings

133
00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.520
time finally locking in something concrete
to anchor the week.

134
00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.400
Daniels message waits unread.

135
00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.800
Leave that for tonight.
Feels like reclaiming.

136
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.800
And we whisper Daniel will wait,

137
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.760
and she thinks the phrase lingers
as background pressure and her thoughts.

138
00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.200
A man's tapping shoe
keeps pace with the hum of bus schedules.

139
00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:10.520
So here we see the whisper
rippling into this person's thoughts.

140
00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:12.640
We click on another character.

141
00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.800
A young woman packs her bag

142
00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:20.040
two rows ahead, methodical, like me
checking the list of bills on the phone.

143
00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:21.640
No sudden moves here.

144
00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:25.280
No surprises, no surprises. Maybe sending.

145
00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:26.400
And we whisper.

146
00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:29.400
There are always surprises.

147
00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.280
The whisper carries the echo of unexpected
turns I can't control.

148
00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.600
Still jotting down the bills tonight.

149
00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:40.920
A habit I trust offers some steady ground
to stand on before Berlin.

150
00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.920
So there's some coherence in there.

151
00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.280
And now for this one.

152
00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:47.960
So here's our ecology.

153
00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.160
I choose the sun.

154
00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:52.160
I shift behind a cloud,
and a cloud appears.

155
00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.320
Now I choose the sun again.

156
00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:56.960
I am warming the earth.

157
00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:58.120
And then I promote

158
00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.920
the growth of a blueberry,
and a blueberry spawns onto the screen.

159
00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.160
I picked the blueberry.

160
00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.080
I am promoted by the sun

161
00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:11.080
and then I produce sweet blueberries.

162
00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:15.240
Well now we did not see a blueberry
appear, so it doesn't always work.

163
00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:16.760
We choose the bug.

164
00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:20.280
I am shaded by the blueberry
and then I crawled beneath its leaves

165
00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:22.120
searching for fallen debris.

166
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.440
I choose the bug again.

167
00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.960
I am shaded by the blueberry
and then I retreat further into the ground

168
00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:34.720
cover, hiding from the watchful eyes
of the owl, and the bug disappears.

169
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.040
And then in what sense? Ripple.

170
00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:40.960
I'm going to let this play, but then pause
just so we can see a tiny detail.

171
00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:46.640
Okay, so what's important here
is that in the interface to begin

172
00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:51.560
interacting, Watson evokes the ripple
through this prompt to drop a stone.

173
00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:57.000
So we dropped the stone,
which in this case is a line of text.

174
00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:00.280
And then as we let it play through,
basically

175
00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:04.040
we get this sequence of sentences
that just go on forever.

176
00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:09.480
So ripples emerge as layered interfaces
tuned, not authored by the performer.

177
00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:14.440
Whispers flow as subtle
shifts, altering collective thought.

178
00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:15.800
Atmospherically,

179
00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.320
ecologies embody

180
00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:22.200
temporary alignments
where user becomes distributed cognition,

181
00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.800
and here you can actually also branch

182
00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:29.320
and choose to take the experience
in different directions.

183
00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.880
And again,
all of these kind of go on infinitely.

184
00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:38.040
So I don't
or we don't really have conclusions,

185
00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:42.440
but rather we decided to kind of list
some questions that emerge

186
00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:47.840
through our practice based process
into exploring this materiality.

187
00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:52.640
So first of our questions
we asked ourselves this a lot.

188
00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.280
How long are we entertained
in reading these?

189
00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:01.680
I remember that Watson at one point
said that he would just play his system

190
00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.640
and just let it play,
much like a radio in the background.

191
00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:10.440
So but then in other times,
it feels like these systems just kind of

192
00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.320
start breaking up, become boring even,

193
00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.640
or start stop making sense.

194
00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:23.680
In my case, I also had to think a lot
about what controls the narrative.

195
00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:28.880
So do I put in a level of variable
states or code,

196
00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:34.760
or is the experience
just based on text that the LM generates?

197
00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:38.760
We also thought about this question.

198
00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:45.120
So can writing become a field in which
actions accumulate rather than disappear?

199
00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:50.000
So where we see consequences
also playing out over time and where

200
00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.040
in a way nothing is lost.

201
00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:57.840
We also struggled a lot
with trying to get the writing

202
00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:02.280
to sound right, because when you write it
yourself, you know what you're going for

203
00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:04.400
and you write it
the way you want it to be written.

204
00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:08.480
But because we were giving up that

205
00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:12.600
or putting that distance between ourselves
and the writing at some time,

206
00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.600
sometimes it became hard
to even understand what it was

207
00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:21.200
that we were looking for in the writing,
and then how to tell the LM to

208
00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:24.600
do what we wanted to do
to make it sound right.

209
00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:28.360
So many of these questions

210
00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:33.240
kind of,
you know, converge on to this question

211
00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:36.920
of what is the proper
distance in distant writing

212
00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.320
and also

213
00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:41.080
this question

214
00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:46.000
of what should the human author contribute
and how should they constrain the LM

215
00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:49.800
in order to produce the most engaging
reading experience,

216
00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.960
and something else that we often
found ourselves orbiting around was

217
00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:58.320
when do you know it's done?

218
00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:02.600
Well, the presentation is done now.
